# Markdown converter

@lede
`brief convert` translates between Markdown and Brief: `.md` in,
canonical Brief out — and `.brf` in, GFM Markdown out. Lossy by design
in both directions; gaps are documented as you go.
@end

## The command

Direction comes from the input extension.

```bash
brief convert notes.md                      # writes notes.brf next to input
brief convert notes.md -o renamed.brf       # rename single output
brief convert notes.md --stdout             # pipe to stdout
brief convert *.md                          # batch; per-file failures don't abort
brief convert notes.brf                     # reverse: writes notes.md
brief convert docs/*.brf                    # batch export to Markdown
```

Per-file failures in batch mode do not abort the run. Each file is
converted independently.

## What translates cleanly

@t
| Markdown | Brief
| `# H1` through `###### H6` | `# H1` through `###### H6`
| `**bold**` | `*bold*`
| `*italic*` or `_italic_` | `_italic_`
| `~~strikethrough~~` | `~strikethrough~`
| `` `code` `` | `` `code` ``
| Triple-backtick fences | Triple-backtick fences (untouched)
| `- item` / `* item` / `+ item` | `- item`
| `1. item` (any starting number) | `1. item` (renumbered to start at 1)
| Tables | `@t` block with `align: [...]` if alignment is present
| Blockquotes | `> ...`

## What's a "design hole"

Constructs without a clean Brief equivalent. The converter:

1. Emits a warning to stderr describing the hole.
2. Writes a `// TODO[B-hole:<kind>]` line at the source location, so the
   hole stays greppable in the converted corpus.

Common holes:

@dl
Inline HTML
: Tags like `<img>`, `<a target="_blank">`, `<div class="...">`. The
  converter does not auto-rewrite — the user decides whether to register
  a custom shortcode (`@image`, `@link`) or to drop the inline.
HTML blocks
: A standalone `<div>...</div>` paragraph. The converter writes a TODO
  line; auto-converting arbitrary HTML is a trap.
YAML frontmatter
: `---` ... `---` at the top of a file. Frontmatter exists in Brief but
  uses TOML inside `+++` delimiters. The converter writes a TODO
  referencing the original frontmatter content.
Wikilinks
: `[[Page]]` syntax. Out of scope for the language; if a docs platform
  built on Brief wants this, it should add a shortcode (e.g. `@wiki[Page]`).
GFM emoji
: `:smile:`. Use plain Unicode. The converter passes through verbatim.
@end

## What gets canonicalized

The converter normalizes constructs that have multiple valid Markdown
spellings to Brief's single canonical form:

- Setext headings (`===` and `---` underlines) become `#`-prefixed
  headings.
- `*` and `+` unordered list markers become `-`.
- Out-of-order ordered list numbering is renumbered to start at 1.
- Reference-style links become `@link[text](url)`.

## What gets transformed

@t
| Markdown | Brief
| `[text](url)` | `@link[text](url)`
| `![alt](src)` | `@image(src: "src", alt: "alt")[]`
| `<kbd>X</kbd>` | `@kbd[X]`
| `<sub>2</sub>` | `@sub[2]`
| `<sup>2</sup>` | `@sup[2]`
| GFM `> [!NOTE]` blocks | `@callout(kind: note) ... @end`
| `- [x] done` | `- [x] done` (passes through)

## When to hand-edit instead

For best results, hand re-write Markdown to Brief. The converter is for
quick wins on a corpus with mostly clean Markdown. A document that leans
heavily on inline HTML, custom HTML blocks, or YAML frontmatter will
need targeted edits anyway — converting first and patching second is
usually faster than rewriting from scratch, but only just.

## The reverse direction

`brief convert doc.brf` exports Brief back to GFM Markdown. It is an
exit ramp for toolchains that only read Markdown — a README that must
render on a forge, a corpus moving into another generator. Brief stays
the canonical form; the `.md` output is a build artifact, not a second
home for your documents.

Wherever GFM offers a syntax the forward converter already reads, the
exporter picks that spelling, so `brf → md → brf` re-converts cleanly:
TOML `+++` frontmatter, `{#anchor}` heading attributes, `> [!NOTE]`
alerts, `term` / `: definition` lists, and footnotes hoisted to `[^n]`
definitions. `@ref[a.brf](title)` becomes `[title](a.md)` — same
relative path, rewritten extension — so a tree exported wholesale keeps
its internal links.

The reverse direction has its own design holes, reported the same way
on stderr; nothing is silently dropped:

@t
| Brief | Markdown | Hole
| `+underline+` | `<u>underline</u>` | `underline-html`
| `//` and `/* */` comments | dropped | `comment-dropped`
| Custom shortcodes | `template_html` expansion, or unwrapped | `custom-shortcode`
| `@nominify` / `@minify` fence attributes | dropped | `code-attrs-dropped`

Strictness is symmetric: the emitted Markdown is fed back through the
Markdown→Brief converter and must compile, or the file is not written.
`--no-strict` opts out. A `.brf` input that does not compile fails the
conversion outright — fix the source first.
